[common]
displayName="DeepSeek Harness"
desc="The open-source, plugin-based coding agent from DeepSeek AI. It serves the DSH Web interface through the fnOS unified gateway and runs on the DSH 0.2.2-rc.1 baseline.<br /><br /><strong>Highlights</strong><br />1. Reach DSH through the NAS Web unified gateway; the service listens on 127.0.0.1 only by default.<br />2. Ships the fnOS plugin; Codex Auth and Failover Search are no longer bundled since 8.1.4 and can be installed from the Discover page or the plugin detail page (versions follow the DSH 0.2.2-rc.1 baseline and compatibility is judged by the new rules); upgrades keep credentials, model settings, sessions and workspaces.<br />3. Grants NAS directory access and remembers authorized directories (kept across upgrades and restarts, pruned automatically once a permission lapses); supports <code>/fn</code> file references, attachments, session log export and opening files in their apps. The fnOS icon above the input area opens a wide panel in place of the command palette, sized to the input box: create folders and empty files, right-click to open or rename an entry (folders open in the file manager, files in fnOS preview), and reopening the panel restores the last folder and scroll position.<br />4. The session log menu matches DSH upstream: under &quot;Download Session log&quot; you can export to your computer or to the NAS, plus a &quot;Feedback&quot; entry pointing at this repository.<br />5. Bundles the <code>fnos-cli</code> Agent Skill (with its own launcher that downloads and verifies the matching CLI version on first use); if you already installed it yourself it is skipped rather than overwritten, and <code>trim-cli</code> is installed only as a global CLI (the <code>trim</code> command); its skill is no longer shipped, and an existing trim-cli skill directory is left untouched.<br />6. Provides an interactive terminal in the sidebar that picks an available bash and injects a UTF-8 environment.<br />7. Proxies third-party plugin API gateways and supports dshmarket; plugins and trim-cli use the npm registry configured in the app's <code>HOME/.npmrc</code>; after an uninstall that keeps data, reinstalling restores the reverse-proxy configuration from cache automatically.<br />8. Publishes a plugin market index: add the source on the DSH &quot;Discover&quot; page to browse and install all seven plugins, with localized names, descriptions and official icons.<br />9. Install, upgrade and runtime configuration stay idempotent: upgrades migrate stale prompt-variable references, and failures keep existing user data; &quot;trusted access address&quot; is validated the same way in the install, upgrade and config wizards and only accepts a host or host:port (several allowed, comma-separated), so a bad value is rejected on the spot instead of surfacing later as &quot;check Node.js dependencies&quot; after an app that will not start.<br /><br /><strong>Shortcuts</strong><br />1. <code>F5</code>, <code>Ctrl + R</code> on Windows/Linux or <code>⌘ + R</code> on macOS: refresh the DSH Web iframe only.<br />2. <code>Ctrl + ,</code> on Windows/Linux or <code>⌘ + ,</code> on macOS: open the DSH settings panel.<br />3. While picking a NAS file under <code>/fn</code>, press <code>Tab</code> to enter a subdirectory.<br />4. In the third-party plugin API URL reverse-proxy settings, press <code>Ctrl/⌘ + S</code> to save.<br /><br /><strong>Installation notes</strong><br />The package comes in x86 and ARM builds with the platform in the file name; pick the one matching your machine (app center refuses to install a package for the wrong platform). The ARM build has not been verified on real hardware by the publisher and is community-tested. The install wizard configures the port, the trusted access address and an npm registry; the listen address is always 127.0.0.1. Enter only a host or host:port for &quot;trusted access address&quot; (for example <code>192.0.2.10:5666</code>), without <code>http://</code> or a path, separated by commas, and wrap IPv6 addresses in brackets. If installation fails, check <code>/var/log/apps/fn-deepseek-harness.log</code> first, keep your existing app data, then retry."
changelog="This release unbundles Codex Auth and Failover Search and adds a self-service repair for failed in-app plugin installs. <strong>Both plugins are no longer bundled</strong>: they are still published and can be installed from the Discover page or a plugin detail page; existing installs, credentials and settings are untouched. <strong>New Repair dependencies action</strong> on the fnOS plugin detail page: a read-only check plus a one-click repair that clears stale publication-age exemptions and rebuilds the Web profile dependencies — the root cause behind pnpm rejecting plugin installs with <code>ERR_PNPM_MINIMUM_RELEASE_AGE_VIOLATION</code>. Only rebuildable lockfiles and dependency directories are cleared; credentials, sessions, workspaces and settings are untouched."

